{:org/id :org-lat-barras-bravas
 :org/name "Barras bravas and torcidas organizadas (Latin American organised supporter groups)"
 :org/entity-kind :org-form
 :org/form-family :family-fan-club
 :org/category :cat-sports
 :org/type :secular-community
 :org/scope :regional
 :org/region-primary :region-south-america
 :org/countries ["AR" "BR" "CO" "CL" "PE" "UY" "MX" "EC"]
 :org/founding-date 1958
 :org/member-count-basis "unknown — the larger barras are described in press and police reporting as having thousands of members with a core of a few hundred, and the Brazilian torcidas organizadas that register as civil associations publish membership in the tens of thousands. Neither figure is auditable, and both are used politically: the barra inflates to bargain with the club, the state inflates to justify policing."
 :org/legal-registration :mixed
 :org/registration-note "Brazilian torcidas organizadas commonly register as civil associations with statutes, headquarters and dues; Argentine and Colombian barras generally do not, and several are formally banned from stadiums. The same form is a registered association on one side of a border and a proscribed group on the other."
 :org/governance-model :hierarchical
 :org/mission-statement "Organised supporter groups that produce the stand — banners, drums, choreography, travel — and in many clubs also control revenue streams (tickets, parking, merchandise, food) and deliver votes in club elections, which is what distinguishes them from the European ultras group."
 :org/coverage-note "Recorded separately from the ultras entry, and recorded with the harm stated: these groups sustain neighbourhood identity and mutual aid for members while also being responsible for deaths, extortion and their capture by club and party patronage. Writing only the first half would be a fan's description, not a record."
 :org/related-org "org-global-ultras"
 :org/data-provenance "llm-recall-unverified"
 :source/dataset "community-coverage"}

{:org/id :org-uru-murga-candombe
 :org/name "Murgas, comparsas and candombe cuerdas (Río de la Plata carnival societies)"
 :org/entity-kind :org-form
 :org/form-family :family-festival-society
 :org/category :cat-traditional
 :org/type :secular-community
 :org/scope :regional
 :org/region-primary :region-south-america
 :org/countries ["UY" "AR"]
 :org/founding-date 1900
 :org/founding-note "Candombe descends from the drumming societies (salas de nación) of enslaved and freed Africans in Montevideo; the barrio cuerdas of Ansina and Cuareim carry that line."
 :org/instance-count-estimate 100
 :org/member-count-basis "unknown — the Montevideo carnival contest registers the groups that compete (murgas, comparsas, parodistas and others, together in the low hundreds), which counts entrants for one season and not the barrio drum lines that never enter, nor the families through whom a drum and a part are inherited."
 :org/legal-registration :mostly-unregistered
 :org/registration-note "Competing groups register with the carnival organisers for the season; the cuerda as a neighbourhood institution is not a registered body and predates the contest."
 :org/governance-model :meritocratic
 :org/mission-statement "Neighbourhood societies that rehearse for months for a forty-night competition — a chorus with a director and a drum line whose three-drum conversation is learned in the street from childhood, and whose lyrics are the year's political commentary."
 :org/coverage-note "Kept distinct from the Caribbean mas camps and the European Narrenzunft: here the transmission line is a barrio and a family, and the displacement of those barrios is what threatens the form."
 :org/related-org "org-car-mas-camps"
 :org/data-provenance "llm-recall-unverified"
 :source/dataset "community-coverage"}

{:org/id :org-mena-zawiya-tariqa
 :org/name "Zawiya and tariqa lodges (Sufi orders of the Maghreb, Levant, Sudan and Turkey)"
 :org/entity-kind :org-form
 :org/form-family :family-religious-lay-committee
 :org/category :cat-traditional
 :org/type :faith-based-org
 :org/scope :regional
 :org/region-primary :region-middle-east
 :org/countries ["MA" "DZ" "TN" "LY" "EG" "SD" "TR" "SY" "IQ"]
 :org/founding-date 1200
 :org/member-count-basis "unknown — orders count neither members nor lodges centrally, and affiliation is a relationship to a shaykh rather than an enrolment. Egyptian Sufi orders are registered with a state council and are described in the millions of adherents; Moroccan and Sudanese figures are estimates from festival attendance."
 :org/legal-registration :mixed
 :org/registration-note "Three regimes at once: Egypt licenses orders through a statutory Supreme Council; Morocco and Algeria recognise and fund zawaya as part of religious policy; and Turkey ABOLISHED the tekke and zaviye by law in 1925, after which the orders continued as associations, foundations and reading circles that are not what they are. A form can be simultaneously state-sponsored in one country and legally non-existent in another."
 :org/governance-model :hierarchical
 :org/mission-statement "Lodges organised around a shaykh and a chain of transmission — hosting travellers, teaching, feeding pilgrims at the annual moussem, mediating local disputes and often holding endowed land — the institution that carried literacy and hospitality across the region before either was a state function."
 :org/related-org "org-sen-dahira"
 :org/data-provenance "llm-recall-unverified"
 :source/dataset "community-coverage"}

{:org/id :org-idn-pesantren-alumni
 :org/name "Pesantren and madrasa alumni networks (ikatan alumni, kyai lineages, NU ranting)"
 :org/entity-kind :org-form
 :org/form-family :family-school-linked
 :org/category :cat-education-parent
 :org/type :faith-based-org
 :org/scope :national
 :org/region-primary :region-southeast-asia
 :org/countries ["ID" "MY" "TH" "PH" "BN"]
 :org/founding-date 1900
 :org/member-count-basis "unknown as a network — Indonesia's religious affairs ministry registers pesantren in the tens of thousands with millions of students, but the alumni network is not the school roll: it is who a graduate keeps answering to decades later, and no one counts that."
 :org/proxy-metric "registered pesantren and enrolled santri"
 :org/proxy-bias "Counts current students at registered schools. The network this entry is about consists of former students, is strongest around the unregistered and small schools, and grows rather than shrinks after graduation."
 :org/legal-registration :mostly-unregistered
 :org/registration-note "The school registers; the alumni network does not, and its authority runs through the kyai and his family rather than through any registered body — which is why it can be mobilised for relief work, business referral or an election without appearing in any register."
 :org/governance-model :hierarchical
 :org/mission-statement "Networks of former boarding-school students bound to a teacher and his lineage — supplying marriage introductions, job referrals, disaster relief volunteers and, at scale, one of Southeast Asia's largest mobilising structures."
 :org/coverage-note "Distinct from the 校友会 / alumni association already recorded: the bond is to a person and his descendants rather than to an institution, and it does not lapse when the school closes."
 :org/related-org "org-global-alumni-associations"
 :org/data-provenance "llm-recall-unverified"
 :source/dataset "community-coverage"}

{:org/id :org-asia-boat-race-crews
 :org/name "Boat race crews and village boat societies (vallam kali snake boats, Bon Om Touk, dragon boat, long-boat crews)"
 :org/entity-kind :org-form
 :org/form-family :family-sport-club
 :org/category :cat-sports
 :org/type :secular-community
 :org/scope :regional
 :org/region-primary :region-asia-south
 :org/countries ["IN" "KH" "TH" "LA" "MM" "CN" "HK" "BD" "ID"]
 :org/founding-date 1600
 :org/member-count-basis "unknown — a single snake boat carries around a hundred rowers and is owned by a village or a group of villages rather than by a club, so the unit that would be counted is a boat and its associated settlements, not a membership. Race organisers count entered boats for one festival."
 :org/proxy-metric "boats entered in a festival race"
 :org/proxy-bias "Counts boats present at one event. A village that could not raise the money to launch this year has not stopped being the boat's community, and a boat rowed by a hired crew counts the same as one rowed by its own village."
 :org/legal-registration :mostly-unregistered
 :org/registration-note "Boat clubs formed for competition register; the older arrangement is that the boat belongs to a temple, a village or a set of families, held under custom with no legal owner in the registry sense."
 :org/governance-model :consensus
 :org/mission-statement "Communities organised around one large boat — building and blessing it, feeding and training a crew of dozens through a season, and racing once a year — an undertaking that requires most of a village and produces its ranking against its neighbours."
 :org/related-org "org-pac-outrigger-canoe-clubs"
 :org/data-provenance "llm-recall-unverified"
 :source/dataset "community-coverage"}
